Z||000000||AL||07-09-2000||02:40 PM||||<BR>i know rodney sells the kit but can anyone tell me what size it is, i'm sure i can rig one up for my car for less. <BR>thanks AL.||||reg||5
Z||000001||Philphine||07-09-2000||06:36 PM||||yep, i made mine. go to auto zone and get a strut with this number:RB8795051. i did this not quite a year ago so i don't remember if that # is on the package, i just went out and got it off the strut. i used a piece of angle iron to brace it near the strut tower on the bracket for the side engine cover. i replaced the top (with the deck open) hinge bolt with a longer one and added a jam nut so i could tighten it on the deck but still give the strut pivot point some free play. i hope that makes sence, i can't do pics but it was pretty easy.<P>the one downside. i read the article on the dickman strut were he said he found one with the right amount of tension for the deck. my strut is the right leanth but is very strong, maybe too strong. i have a saleen mustang wing on my deck and it pretty much slams my deck up any way. i hold it when i open it just to be on the safe side. like i said, i did this not quite a year ago and no damage but i still try to be careful. i don't know if i'd try it with an electric trunk release, even though if you just turn the key it stays down until you open it so far. if you have access to various struts maybe you can experiment. i pretty much went on lenth and how the ends were made.<P>oh yeah cost. not much more than the price of the strut if you have about a 2" piece of 1" angle iron and a few nuts and bolts. the bolt for the hinge i think was metric.<P>if you make to the louisville show and I make it (in the car ), i'll give up some pride and reveal my filthy neglected engine compartment (have i mentioned i hate my engine?) and let you take a look at it. or maybe some one will photo it.   ||||reg||1
