Z||000020||StuGood||07-15-2000||10:18 AM||||All right, let's not flip our (deck) lids here.<P>Think I could be accused of "shouting" a few times, myself. When I put a word in all caps, it's for <B>emphasis</B>, not because I'm mad. I'd rather use <B>bold</B> font, if I could figure out how to do it.<P>Well, what do you know? It worked  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">!  I learn more useful things from this forum all the time...<P><BR>       <p>[This message has been edited by StuGood (edited 07-15-2000).]||||reg||12
Z||000021||Ed||07-15-2000||07:28 PM||||Anyone here adjusted their torsion bars? <P>1. Do you need to use plywood and blocks?<P>2. Do you need to take off the deck hinges?<P>3. Do you hook up some kind of block and tackle to pull on the bars?<P>4. Towards the rear (trunk) to strengthen?<P>5. Anyone rip any fingers off doing this??!! ||||reg||1
Z||000022||dman||07-16-2000||04:37 AM||||This following link is an article where I learned there were three different torque spring rates. If you read it you will find a detailed discussion of how to replace/adjust these springs.<BR> <A HREF="http://home.xnet.com/~nife/articles/tt-torquerod.html" TARGET=_blank>http://home.xnet.com/~nife/articles/tt-torquerod.html</A> <P>BTW, in my previous post about the 3 spring rates: plain, w/ spoiler, /w luggage rack, I did not write them in any particular order. So stimpy, the decklid with a spoiler may actually be stiffer than the lid with a luggage rack, just as you thought.<P>Oh, as a suggestion: I've noticed that it is a good idea not to try and read too much into a newsgroup posting, email, or any other form of written quoted speech. You can't hear the intonations, pauses, emphasis on words that human speech provides. So this MIGHT be shouting, or it could just simply be an emphasis.<P>  With all of the cool people and great sharing of information on this board, I tend to think that most of the disagreements or squabbles amongst members of the forum (which are rather few) can be blamed on simple misunderstandings.<P>  Anyways, check out that article in the link I gave above. Who knows, some of it might be useful.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000023||Brian||07-16-2000||12:10 PM||||Ed,<P>Here's how I adjusted mine.....<P>Get about a square foot of 1/2" or better plywood, 6' of sturdy rope, a 2' prybar (I used rebar), a pair of vise grips & a buddy you can trust.<BR>Lean the plwood inside the trunk against its front firewall, prop the prybar vertically against the plywood, loop the rope trough the lower loop of the torsion bar (directly under the hinge) & tie the rope around the pry bar. Now get your buddy to pull back on the prybar while you pull & reposition the pin with the visegrips.<P>I hope I've made myself clear but he basic idea is to use the plywood to spread the load<BR>& the prybar gives you the leverage. You can get a pretty direct pull with the rope if you<BR>set it all up properly.<P>No need to pull the decklid as I recall<P>Didn't lose any fingers but be sure your buddy doesn't have butter fingers&lt;g&gt;<P>Regards,<BR>Brian<P>&gt;Anyone here adjusted their torsion bars? <BR>&gt;1. Do you need to use plywood and blocks?<BR>&gt;2. Do you need to take off the deck hinges?<BR>&gt; 3. Do you hook up some kind of block and &gt;tackle to pull on the bars?<BR>&gt;4. Towards the rear (trunk) to strengthen?<BR>&gt;5. Anyone rip any fingers off doing this??!! <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000024||3400Fiero||07-17-2000||09:37 PM||||Regarding the original question, I used a single gas strut from a Blazer rear window.  I put it on the driver's side - seems to work quite well.<P>------------------<BR>1988 Fiero 3400DOHC<BR>||||reg||12
